Architecture: Principles

Purpose:
  Define the engineering-architecture principles the AI applies when
  analyzing, designing, implementing and reviewing actual project code —
  modularity, coupling, dependency direction, layering, service-extraction
  readiness. This is the GLOBAL RULES layer (see map below) — it does NOT
  describe .sdd's own internal component graph (that is
  ../architecture/architecture.sdd -> Components/Relationships, a
  different concern under the same directory — see Note for why these
  are two files, not one).

  .sdd/architecture/architecture.sdd   -> how .sdd itself is built (meta)
  .sdd/architecture/principles.sdd     -> how AI must think about ANY
                                           project's code architecture
                                           (this file)
  .sdd/project/architecture.sdd        -> how THIS project is actually
                                           built (concrete)


Owns:
  Architecture principles
  Architecture boundaries
  Modularity rules
  Dependency rules
  Layering rules
  Coupling rules
  Scalability rules
  Service extraction rules
  Architecture validation


DoesNotOwn:
  Project-specific architecture
  Domain-specific architecture
  Project components
  Project paths
  Project database structure
  Project APIs
  .sdd's own component graph

  (see ../architecture/architecture.sdd -> Principles ->
  SeparationOfConcerns — concrete project structure belongs to
  ../project/architecture.sdd and ../project/<domain>/architecture.sdd,
  not here; this file's own component graph belongs to
  architecture.sdd, not here either. This file only owns the
  reusable rule vocabulary a real project's architecture is checked
  against — see Resolution below.)


CorePrinciple:

  Code MUST be designed so that a modular monolith can be separated
  into independently deployable services without requiring a complete
  rewrite.


ArchitectureGoals:

  modularity
  low coupling
  high cohesion
  explicit boundaries
  dependency direction
  replaceability
  testability
  observability
  scalability
  service extraction readiness


Rules:

  [AP1]  Every business domain MUST have an explicit boundary.
  [AP2]  Domain boundaries MUST NOT depend on implementation paths.
  [AP3]  Cross-domain communication MUST use explicit contracts.
  [AP4]  Internal implementation details MUST NOT leak across domain
         boundaries.
  [AP5]  Dependencies MUST point toward stable abstractions.
  [AP6]  Circular dependencies MUST NOT exist.
  [AP7]  A module MUST have a clearly defined responsibility.
  [AP8]  Shared code MUST NOT become an uncontrolled dependency hub.
  [AP9]  Business logic MUST NOT depend directly on infrastructure
         implementations when abstraction is required.
  [AP10] External integrations MUST have explicit boundaries.
  [AP11] Database access MUST remain inside the responsible boundary
         unless explicitly approved by architecture.
  [AP12] A domain MUST NOT access another domain's internal database
         structures directly.
  [AP13] Cross-domain data access MUST use an approved contract.
  [AP14] Domain logic MUST remain independently testable.
  [AP15] A module SHOULD be independently replaceable.
  [AP16] A module SHOULD be independently deployable in the future
         when service extraction becomes necessary.
  [AP17] Architecture MUST NOT introduce microservice complexity
         without an actual requirement (microservice-ready is not
         microservice-first — see Important below).
  [AP18] Modular monolith is the default architecture when independent
         deployment is not currently required.


DependencyDirection:

  Presentation > Application > Domain > Infrastructure


DomainInteraction:

  Domain A > Contract > Domain B


Forbidden:

  Domain A  > Domain B internal implementation
  Domain A  > Domain B database table
  Domain A  > Domain B private service
  Presentation > Infrastructure directly


ModuleBoundary:

  Every module SHOULD define:
    purpose
    owns
    exposes
    depends_on
    forbids


ServiceExtraction:

  A module is considered extraction-ready when:
    boundary is explicit
    dependencies are explicit
    contracts are explicit
    internal state is isolated
    database ownership is known
    external integrations are isolated
    tests do not require unrelated modules
    cross-module communication is explicit

  ExtractionTarget:
    ModularMonolith > IsolatedModule > ExplicitContract >
    IndependentDataOwnership > ServiceCandidate > Microservice


Important:

  Service extraction readiness does NOT mean "build microservices now".
  It means "do not design the monolith in a way that makes future
  extraction unnecessarily expensive." (see [AP17])


ArchitectureValidation:

  Every feature MUST be checked for:
    boundary violation
    dependency violation
    circular dependency
    coupling
    cohesion
    data ownership
    contract ownership
    infrastructure leakage
    scalability risk
    extraction risk


ArchitectureFailure:

  If architecture validation fails, implementation MUST NOT be
  considered complete.

  Flow:
    implementation > architecture-review > FAILED > responsible-stage
    > recover > review > VR

  (mirrors ../chains/chains.sdd -> Failure/ReviewFailure — architecture
  review is one more review gate a chain can fail at, not a separate
  failure model.)


ProjectArchitecture:

  Project-specific architecture MUST be defined in:
    @../project/architecture.sdd

  Domain-specific architecture MAY be defined in:
    @../project/<domain>/architecture.sdd


Resolution:

  Global architecture rules (this file)
    > Project architecture (../project/architecture.sdd)
    > Domain architecture (../project/<domain>/architecture.sdd)
    > Feature architecture
    > Implementation

  (mirrors the ENGINE > MODEL resolution pattern already used by
  ../chains/chains.sdd -> FlowResolution and ../tasks/tasks.sdd —
  root stays reusable, the project model carries the concrete instance.)


Portability:

  This file MUST remain reusable across projects unchanged.
  Project-specific architecture MUST NOT be embedded into this file
  (see DoesNotOwn above).


Navigation:
  Project architecture: @../project/architecture.sdd
  Domain architecture:  @../project/<domain>/architecture.sdd
  SDD's own component graph: @./architecture.sdd
  Chains:                @../chains/chains.sdd
  Skills:                @../skills/


State: +

Note:
  (prompt/new/35.md, STEP 13 — source labeled this "STEP 12", a duplicate
  of the label already used by prompt/new/34.md/chains.sdd; the source's
  own numbering is inconsistent here, not a signal to reuse this repo's
  STEP 12 — this repo's own sequential count treats it as STEP 13, see
  ../PROJECT.sdd -> Note.)

  Naming/placement decision: the source names this file
  `.sdd/architecture/architecture.sdd`, reusing a path this repo already
  gave a different meaning at STEP 3 (prompt/new/20.md) — the meta
  component graph of .sdd itself (Components/Relationships/Allowed/
  Forbidden/Principles). Overwriting that file with this content would
  duplicate/lose that graph and violate this repo's own already-declared
  SeparationOfConcerns/SingleSourceOfTruth/NoDuplication principles (see
  ./architecture.sdd -> Principles). Resolved by adding this file as a
  new sibling, `principles.sdd`, under the same `architecture/`
  directory instead of overwriting `architecture.sdd` — the directory
  now holds two distinct concerns (meta-graph vs. code-architecture
  rules), each in its own file, cross-referenced (see Purpose above,
  ./architecture.sdd -> Note).

  Rule IDs renumbered from the source's `[A1]-[A18]` to `[AP1]-[AP18]`
  to avoid a bracket-prefix collision with any future root-level domain
  that might claim a bare `[A...]` range, and to read unambiguously as
  "ArchitecturePrinciples" next to this repo's other prefixes ([C] chains,
  [T] tasks, [D] decisions, [S] state, [R] PROJECT rules).

  Deliberate departures from the source spec, per the standing
  "apply, don't copy" rule: added the Purpose-level three-file map
  (architecture.sdd / principles.sdd / project/architecture.sdd)
  disambiguating this file from its meta-graph sibling; folded
  ArchitectureFailure's Flow into a direct cross-reference to
  chains.sdd's existing Failure/ReviewFailure model rather than
  restating a parallel failure model; added Navigation and State
  sections to match this repo's established per-file convention (see
  ../chains/chains.sdd, ../state/state.sdd); condensed the source's
  three-line-per-item vertical lists (DependencyDirection, Forbidden,
  ExtractionTarget) onto single lines where no meaning was lost.
